<SubSection title = "Four-bar linkages" id = "four_bar_linkages">
  <Image src = "/dyn/multi_body_systems/Crank-Rocker_4-bar_Linkage.gif" width = "5">Image from <a target = "_blank" rel = "noopener noreferrer" href = "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crank-Rocker_4-bar_Linkage.gif">Wikimedia.</a> CC BY 2.0</Image>
  <p>
    A four-bar linkage is a common mechanism that is the simplest closed-chain movable linkage.
    It consists of four members, connected in a loop by four joints.
    One member is typically fixed to the ground by pin supports.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is is something to keep in mind when solving problems involving four-bar linkages:
  </p>
  <ul>
      <li>The tangential velocity of the points at the pins is zero.</li>
  </ul>
  <CalloutContainer slot = "cards">
    <CalloutCard title = "Application alert!">
      <a href = "/dyn/four_bar_linkages_applications">Four-bar linkages applications</a> uses <strong>four-bar-linkages</strong> by going far more in depth into specific instances of four-bar linkages.
      <PrairieDrawCanvas id = "aml-ft-c" width = "250" height = "250"/>
      <p>
        Four-bar linkages are found in many mechanical systems, including pumpjacks, bicycles, and suspensions.
      </p>
    </CalloutCard>
    <CalloutCard title = "Heads up!">
      <p>
        <strong>4 bar linkage</strong> builds on this content in <a href = "/md/Planar_mechanisms#4%20bar%20linkage">mechanism design.</a>
      </p>
      <Image src = "/dyn/multi_body_systems/4Bar.png" width = "6"/>
      <p>
        Four-bar linkages are one of the most common mechanisms.
      </p>
    </CalloutCard>
  </CalloutContainer>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title = "Slider cranks"  id = "slider_cranks">
  <Image src = "/dyn/multi_body_systems/Slider_Crank_animation.svg" width = "5">Image from <a target = "_blank" rel = "noopener noreferrer" href = "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slider_Crank_animation.svg">Wikimedia.</a> CC BY 2.0</Image>
  <p>
    A slider crank mechanism is a common mechanical system that converts rotational motion into linear motion.
    It consists of a crank, a connecting rod, and a slider.
    The crank rotates about a fixed axis, the connecting rod connects the crank to the slider, and the slider moves linearly along a guide or track.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here are some things to keep in mind when solving problems involving slider-crank mechanisms:
  </p>
  <ul>
      <li>The velocity of the slider is purely tangential to the path of the slider.</li>
      <li>The acceleration of the slider is purely tangential to the path of the slider.</li>
			<li>The slider has a reaction force normal to the path of the slider.</li>
  </ul>
  <CalloutContainer slot = "cards">
    <CalloutCard title = "Review?">
      <p>
        Need a review of <strong>types of connections?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
